Certainly! In LaTeX, a table of contents (TOC) is a tool that automatically generates a list of sections and their corresponding page numbers for easy navigation within your document. Here's an example of how to create an automatically generated table of contents in LaTeX:

1. Begin your LaTeX document by adding the `\documentclass{}` command, specifying the document class (e.g., `article`, `report`, or `book`). This will help LaTeX understand the structure of your document.

2. Within the document preamble (everything before the `\begin{document}` command), add the necessary packages for the features you want to use. For creating an automatically generated table of contents, you will need the `tocloft` package. Here's an example:

   ```latex
   \documentclass{article}
   \usepackage[titles]{tocloft} % Required for creating a custom ToC
   \usepackage{lipsum} % Required for generating dummy text
   \begin{document}
   ```

   The `tocloft` package provides additional customization options for the table of contents. If you prefer the default table of contents, you can omit this line.

3. Add a document title (enclosed between `\title{}` and `\maketitle`) if desired.

4. Create the sections of your document, enclosed between numbered section commands (e.g., `\section{Section 1}`, `\section{Section 2}`, etc.). The specific sectioning commands you use depend on the document class you specified in Step 1. Here are some examples:

   For an `article` document class:

   ```latex
   \section{Introduction}
   \lipsum[1]
   \section{First Section}
   \lipsum[2]
   \section{Second Section}
   \lipsum[3]
   \section{Conclusion}
   \lipsum[4]
   \end{document}
   ```

   For a `report` document class:

   ```latex
   \documentclass{report}
   \begin{document}
   \tableofcontents % Generate the table of contents
   \chapter{Introduction}
   \lipsum[1]
   \chapter{First Chapter}
   \lipsum[2]
   \chapter{Second Chapter}
   \lipsum[3]
   \chapter{Conclusion}
   \lipsum[4]
   \end{document}
   ```

   For a `book` document class:

   ```latex
   \documentclass{book}
   \begin{document}
   \tableofcontents % Generate the table of contents
   \chapter{Introduction}
   \lipsum[1]
   \chapter{Part One}
   \chapter{First Chapter of Part One}
   \chapter{Second Chapter of Part One}
   \chapter{Conclusion of Part One}
   \lipsum[2]
   \chapter{Part Two}
   \chapter{First Chapter of Part Two}
   \chapter{Second Chapter of Part Two}
   \chapter{Conclusion of Part Two}
   \lipsum[3]
   \chapter{Epilogue}
   \lipsum[4]
   \end{document}
   ```

   Note that the specific sectioning commands used depend on the document class specified in Step 1.

5. Compile your LaTeX document. The first time you compile your LaTeX document, there will be no table of contents yet. To generate the table of contents, include the `\tableofcontents` command (with a backslash) at the location in your document where you want the table of contents to appear. Common locations for the table of contents include:

   - Immediately after the `\begin{document}` command (as in the `report` example above).
   - Before the first sectioning command (as in the `article` example above).
   - Using a dedicated `toc` float environment (create this environment using the `\begin{toc}` command). This approach allows for additional formatting options using the `tocloft` package.

6. Continue editing your LaTeX document as desired. Add, remove, or rearrange sections as needed.

7. Re-compile your LaTeX document to see the table of contents generated from the sectioning commands added in Step 4. The table of contents will include the section titles and their corresponding page numbers.

Here's an example of what your complete LaTeX document might look like:

```latex
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage[titles]{tocloft}
\usepackage{lipsum}
\begin{document}
\tableofcontents

\section{Introduction}
\lipsum[1]

\section{First Section}
\lipsum[2]

\section{Second Section}
\lipsum[3]

\section{Conclusion}
\lipsum[4]

\end{document}
```

This will generate the following table of contents:

```
Contents
--------
Introduction                            1
First Section                           2
Second Section                          3
Conclusion                             4
```

To create a LaTeX code to display a chess board in FEN notation, you will need to use the `chessboard` package, which provides commands to easily generate a chess board based on FEN notation.

Here's an example of a LaTeX code that takes a FEN string as input and generates the corresponding chess board:

```
\documentclass{article}

\usepackage{xcolor} % for color manipulation
\usepackage{chessboard} % for chess board and notation

\newcommand{\PrintBoard}[1]{%
  \Large
  \def\cbw{\chessboardsetup{#1}} % store the configuration
  \cbstartsetup\cbw % initialize the configuration
  \noindent\chessboard % print the board
  \par % optional paragraph break
}

\begin{document}

% Sample FEN string
\newcommand{\myfen}{rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq}

% Print the chess board in FEN notation
\PrintBoard{\myfen}

\end{document}
```

Let's go through the code step by step:

1. Load the required LaTeX packages: 

   - `xcolor`: This package provides additional color options, which we'll need to customize the color of the chess pieces.

2. Define a new command `\PrintBoard` that takes a single argument, the FEN string.

3. Within the `\PrintBoard` command:

   a. Set up the board configuration using `\chessboardsetup`. This command parses the FEN string and stores the necessary information to render the board.

   b. Initialize the configuration with the stored information using `\cbstartsetup\cbw`.

   c. Use `\noindent` to prevent an unwanted paragraph break before the board.

   d. Finally, use `\chessboard` to generate the chess board based on the stored configuration.

4. In the document scope, call your custom command with your desired FEN string.

Here's an explanation of the FEN string used in the example:

- `rnbqkbnr`: Position of the white pieces on the starting rank (2nd rank).
- `/`: Separator between the squares on the starting rank.
- `pppppppp`: Position of the white pieces on the 3rd rank.
- `/`: Rank separator.
- `8`: Blank 4th rank.
- `/`: Rank separator.
- `8`: Blank 5th rank.
- `/`: Rank separator.
- `8`: Blank 6th rank.
- `/`: Rank separator.
- `pppppppp`: Position of the black pieces on the 7th rank.
- `/`: Rank separator.
- `rnbqkbnr`: Position of the black pieces on the starting rank (2nd rank).
- `w`: Color of the move (white).
- ` KQkq`: Castle rights (K for short white king, Q for short black queen, k and q indicate the respective castling sides).

In the provided code, the resulting output will be a black opening position with standard chess rules and notations.

If you only provide the FEN string without the board configuration, you can still use the `\chessboard` command by removing the `\PrintBoard{\myfen}` line and replacing it with:

```
\chessboard{\myfen}
```

This will generate the same chess board, but you'll need to include the required packages (`chessboard` and `xcolor`) at the beginning of your LaTeX document. 

If you want to customize the color of the pieces, you can use the `chessboard/setupa` package option and define your own color scheme. Here's an example:

```
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{xcolor}
\usepackage[chessboard,chessboard/setupa]{chessboard}

\newcommand{\PrintBoard}[1]{
  \cbsetupa{piece-style={WN=, BB=}}
  \chessboard{#1}
}

\begin{document}

\newcommand{\myfen}{rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq}

\PrintBoard{\myfen}

\end{document}
```

This code will produce a chess board with white pieces represented by a crown symbol (``) instead of the default white pawn (`P`) and knight (`N`) symbols. You'll need to define your own symbols for the black pieces using a similar syntax (`BB=` becomes a black bishop, for example) if you want to customize both colors. 

Note that the `chessboard/setupa` option replaces the default setup, so you'll need to specify all the piece symbols you want to customize. You can find a list of the default symbols in the `chessboard` documentation. 

Also, if you want to customize the board background, you can use the `frame=` option when calling `\chessboard`. For example, `\chessboard[frame=tt]{\myfen}` will draw a box around the board using the `tt` font to create the vertical and horizontal lines. You can also use the `board-size=` option to adjust the size of the board.  Here's an example:

```
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{xcolor}
\usepackage[chessboard,chessboard/setupa]{chessboard}

\newcommand{\PrintBoard}[1]{
  \cbsetupa{
    piece-style={
      WN=, BB=,
      frame=tt,
      board-size=12
    },
    showmover=false % hide the move configuration for the example
  }
  \chessboard{#1}
}

\begin{document}

\newcommand{\myfen}{r3k2r/1p1p1p1/2p2p/pP2PpP/P1P2P2/8/1P1P1P1/R3K2R w KQkq}

\PrintBoard{\myfen}

\end{document}
```

This code will produce a larger chess board (with size 12 instead of 8) with white pieces represented by a crown symbol (``) instead of the default white pawn and knight symbols. The board will also be framed using a `tt` font. The example FEN string is a simplified version of the starting position with some minor piece and pawn rearrangements. The `showmover=false` option is used to hide the move configuration (e.g., `e2e4`) since it's not required for the example. If you want to display a move, you can pass the move configuration as a string after the FEN, separated by a space. For example, `\PrintBoard{\myfen e2e4}` will display the move `e2e4` on the resulting board.

